From 3ff594cc4f1267dc0843b6d05c94e88f33d8b789 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 23:39:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 80/88] dns/fakeip: answer new names from memory while the
 cache file cannot be written

With a cache file the store gave out addresses only within a reserve saved ahead (1024
addresses) and, in a full range, only after the records that made room had left the file.
When the file could not be written (no space left on the overlay, a file system remounted
read-only, a damaged cache file that could not be reopened) the reserve ran out and every
new name got an error instead of an address, at once when the first save at start had
failed: the query had no answer and the error was logged per query, although the whole
table is in memory. It lasted until the file could be written again.

The store now remembers that the last write failed and, until one succeeds, gives out
addresses past the saved reserve and lets a full range make room in memory. The records
stay pending and reach the file, with a new reserve, with the first write that succeeds;
the records that made room meanwhile are removed from the file by the same write. Create
no longer returns the error of a save: the background loop logs the failure once and
retries every second, as before.

The price is stated in the comment of Store: a crash or a kill while the file cannot be
written is not covered by the reserve. An address given out past it can be given to
another domain after the restart, and an address that made room can lead to its previous
domain. Addresses that had reached the file keep their guarantee.
